              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In broad generality, under
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-categories
            one understands
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+categories"} higher categories
            with non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+morphisms"} higher morphisms
            up to dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N} \sqcup \{\infty\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            these are the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            (or
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-categories"} 1-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
            they are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
            (traditionally: “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategories"}> bicategories
            ”) and/or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
            one also speaks of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategories"} tricategories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \infty
            would broadly refer to the most general notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-categories"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-categories
            where there is no constraint on the dimensionality of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+morphisms"} higher morphisms
          %p
            Especially as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            increases, there is a plethora of different definitions of
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ListOfDefinitions"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            is to understand, organize, systematize and, last not least, apply these definitions. (It is the “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            ” in “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            \-category” that gives the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HomePage"} nLab
            its name.)
          %p
            Often it is important to consider the more fine-grained notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-categories"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k,n)
                  \-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            refers only to the maximal dimension of non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+morphisms"} invertible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+morphisms"} higher morphisms
            (non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences"}> equivalences
            ) while the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+morphisms"} invertible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+morphisms"} higher morphisms
            are allowed to range in dimension up to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N} \sqcup \{\infty\}
          %p
            Particularly important here is the limiting case (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = \infty
            ) of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-categories"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
                \-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C0%29-categories"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,0)
              \-categories
            aka
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoids"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-groupoids
            are the bare
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            of “spaces” as considered in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-categories"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
              \-categories
            are the natural notion of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            (also: internal to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+laws"} coherence laws
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            typically have a particularly natural (if maybe non-explicit) formulation. Therefore many authors these days use “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-category” as a pseudonym for
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                \-category
            and some use “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            \-category” as a tacit pseudonym for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-categories"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
                \-categories
          %p
            In this
            = succeed "-perspective" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+spaces"} directed spaces
            (with an
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+theory"} directed homotopy theory
            (and
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+type+theory"} directed homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-categories"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
              \-category
            theory, though a genuine perspective of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+theory"} directed homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+type+theory"} directed homotopy type theory
            is still in its infancy.
          %p
            Conversely, for concrete computations it is at times convenient to have
            %em less
            flexible and more rigid definitions or at least presentations/models of
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-strict+n-categories"} semi-strict n-categories
            and much of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            revolves around identifying and comparing such
            = succeed "." do
              %em rigidifications
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+n-categories"}
                strict
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            are typically too rigid to play more than a niche role in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %p
            Finally, it is sometimes useful to subsume degenerate cases in the general pattern of
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-categories"} 0-categories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"}> sets
            ),
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-categories"} (0,1)-categories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"}> posets
            ) and even
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-categories"} (-1)-categories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"}> truth values
            ) and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-2%29-categories"} (-2)-categories
            (the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} singleton category
          %h2#categories_of_categories
            Categories of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Just as the collection of all (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"}> small
            ) sets is the prototypical example of a category, so the collection of all small
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
          %p
            Actually, if you define things cleverly, then you can get an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %em all
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Axiom of Universes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0 \subset U_1 \subset U_2 \subset \cdots
          %p
            and we can say a set is
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              \-small
            if it is an element of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set
              is the category of all
              = succeed "-small" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Cat
              is the 2-category of all
              = succeed "-small" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\Cat
              is the 3-category of all
              = succeed "-small" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
            %li etc.
          %p
            This is a convenient way to settle size questions once and for all for finite
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            For more, see the discussion at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://groups.google.com/group/sci.logic/browse_thread/thread/6891c85c2d9b2caf"} sci.logic
          %h2#defn Definitions
          %p
            Here is a list of (some of) the proposed definitions of (weak)
            = succeed "-category," do
