                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
                and generalized it to a definition of
                = succeed "-category;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a{:href => "http://people.math.jussieu.fr/~maltsin/ps/infgrart.pdf"} this
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://people.math.jussieu.fr/~maltsin/ps/infctart.pdf"} this
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simona+Paoli"}> Simona Paoli
                \:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+globular+n-fold+categories"} weakly globular n-fold categories
          %h3#comparisons Comparisons
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                All definitions produce the correct well-known notion of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
            %li
              %p
                Since all the common definitions of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
                are known to be equivalent (give references!), the definitions of Street, Trimble, Tamsamani–Simpson, Joyal, Barwick, and Rezk can be said to agree for
                = succeed "-categories." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %li
              %p
                Julie Bergner has
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0710.2254"} shown
                that all the notions of
                = succeed "-groupoid" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                = succeed "-categories." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,0)
            %li
              %p
                It is known that the notions of
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,0)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-types"} homotopy n-types
                for
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≤
                        %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\le 3
            %li
              %p
                In Tom Leinster’s paper, proofs are sketched showing that the notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                obtained in each case looks somewhat like the notion of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %li
              %p
                Nick Gurski has shown in “Nerves of bicategories as stratified simplicial sets” that Street’s definition is correct for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
                (that is, it agrees with bicategories).
            %li
              %p
                Eugenia Cheng has
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0304285"} shown
                that the opetopic definition is correct for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
                (that is, it agrees with bicategories).
            %li
              %p
                Eugenia Cheng has more recently also
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0809.2070"} shown
                that from any sequence of operads used for iterated enrichment in a Trimble-style definition, one can construct a Batanin–Leinster-style globular operad whose algebras are the
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
